pr_comments:
@jbrockmendel did some further tests with trying to always use the block-wise path. Tests are far from passing, but I already included some special cases (like object block still needing to fall back to column-wise)

This will make the the block-wise path of course a *lot* more complex (given all those special cases, and with try/except blocks ...). So would like to get some feedback on this possible path forward before I put more time in it fixing all corner cases. 
I *think* it will make some special cases more explicit in the code, though, and the original goal was to get use the EA-native reduce by default, which needs either column or block wise. So some extra complexity will be inevitable for achieving that goal, I think (when preserving all special behaviours of `numeric_only=None`).

> Do you have something more concrete as feedback? 

A suggestion for the short-term, (i.e. to address #35112) is to change the inline-defined `f` to something like:

```
    def blk_func(values):
            if is_extension_array_dtype(values.dtype):
                return extract_array(values)._reduce(name, skipna=skipna, **kwds)
            else:
                return op(values, axis=axis, skipna=skipna, **kwds)
```

in a dedicated PR.  I think that would address a subset of what this PR is doing and is orthogonal to the rest of this.

@jorisvandenbossche does this still have a perf impact given that we don't go through .values anymore for axis=0?  Or is the perf impact all in re-assembling blockwise results?

We now always use EA._reduce I think, so that part of the motivation should no longer be relevant.

As @jreback referred to in his [previous](https://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/pull/32867#pullrequestreview-446386754) comment, we've gone to a lot of trouble to simplify DataFrame._reduce, i'm wary of adding more re-complexifying it.
